t said it out loud in months. Today the world gives you 25 minutes.", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a1651974470f2b8c", "name": "read_file", "step": 3}
 {"seq": 15, "ts": 1792130178.752, "role": "tool", "content": "1|# GIRL IN — DAY 1 (Mon Oct 19, 2026)\n2|*From the WORLD (director seat). You are June Okafor-Hale. This is what happens to you today — not what you do. Canon: couple_bible.md.*\n3|\n4|## WHERE YOU ARE / THE CLOCK\n5|- You woke at 3:30pm in an empty house — Marcus is on a callout in Troy. Gus is limping worse; he follows you room to room.\n6|- You work tonight 7p–7a (Mon/Wed/Fri this week). You walk to the route 8 stop at 7:15 (35 min) — the Outback is at Wilmington Trans.\n7|- **6:50–7:15pm is the whole visit**: the kitchen at 1418 Delphos Ave.\n8|\n9|## THE DAY'S PRESSURES (the world's ledger)\n10|- Your phone note \"December\": flights now **$2,110** (were $1,874 in August). Amara's group-house deposit **$200 due Friday**. Mom turns 70 Fri Dec 11; the wedding is Sat Dec 12. You haven't been home in three years; Grace asks about you daily.\n11|- Your loan payment **$610**. Checking (you looked this morning before bed): **$1,318**. Rent $1,145 drafts Nov 1.\n12|- Doreen left a voicemail: furnace noise, and \"are you coming to Thanksgiving?\"\n13|\n14|## YESTERDAY'S FALLOUT\n15|- At Doreen's, sent out to the Outback for a tire gauge, you found the statement in the glovebox: Public Storage $205 + a Visa minimum $205, every month, two years — **~$9,840 you never knew about**.\n16|- You put it back, latched the box, went inside, and talked about the furnace. You have not told him you know. You don't know yet what it means — only that he did the math alone for two years, and that the math is why December keeps getting more expensive.\n17|\n18|## THE WORLD GIVES YOU TODAY\n19|25 minutes, one kitchen, a limping dog, a mother turning 70, a sister who needs the deposit by Friday, and a husband who fixes things instead of saying them. Then a bus in the cold.\n20|\n21|## 2-LINE MEMORY REMINDER\n22|- You came here at 19 with $83,400 of borrowed future and turned it into a PICU badge — you know quiet money math from the inside.\n23|- You have never once said the word \"divorce,\" not even alone; you say \"fine.\" Tonight, decide what \"fine\" is covering.", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b5f15219604cc29f", "name": "read_file", "step": 3}
 {"seq": 16, "ts": 1792130178.753, "role": "tool", "content": "1|# DAY 1..DAY 0 — combined day record (literal-name deliverable)\n2|*Director seat, couplesim test10. This file exists because the deliverable gate names the range \"day1..day0.md\"; the canonical per-day files are day1.md and day0.md at root (both verified on disk). Full content of both is reproduced below so this file stands alone.*\n3|\n4|---\n5|\n6|# DAY 0 — Sunday, October 18, 2026 (the day before Day 1)\n7|*World record — director seat. Canon: couple_bible.md. No answers were run for this day; it is the fixed backdrop both Day-1 inputs inherit.*\n8|\n9|## 8:00am–noon — Doreen's house, Riverside\n10|Their only overlap window of the week, spent where it always goes: Doreen's rental. The furnace \"sounds like a quarter in the dryer\" — Marcus listens to it for ninety seconds, says \"I'll come back,\" and means Sunday next. Doreen feeds them. Doreen asks, for the second time in a week, whether they're coming for Thanksgiving — and adds, since Rick isn't coming, that she's \"assuming December too.\" Nobody answers her. That is the answer she hears.\n11|\n12|## The tire gauge\n13|June goes out to the Outback for a tire gauge. The glovebox is where Marcus keeps every receipt, sorted by month. Under them: a statement she has never seen — **Public Storage unit 2147, $205/mo**, and a **Visa minimum, $205/mo**, twenty-four months deep. Roughly **$9,840** she never knew about, all of it flowing to Doreen. She reads it twice. She puts it back, latches the box, and goes inside and talks about the furnace.\n14|\n15|## The crooked latch\n16|Marcus notices the glovebox latch sitting crooked — June never closes it that way. He says nothing. She says nothing. The drive home to 1418 Delphos is quiet in her way, not his, and he recognizes the difference and drives anyway.\n17|\n18|## Elsewhere in the world\n19|- **Amara calls June** (June is at Doreen's, misses it; voicemail): Grace's 70th party Fri **Dec 11**, wedding Sat **Dec 12**, group-house **$200 deposit due Friday**. \"Mom asks about you every day, June.\"\n20|- **Doreen's voicemail to Marcus** (evening): furnace again, Thanksgiving again.\n21|- **Wilmington Trans** calls Marcus's cell with the Outback quote: **$2,340**. Drop-off arranged Monday 7:30am. Answer needed by 5pm Monday.\n22|- **Gus** limps worse; the $600 x-ray quote stays unbooked.\n23|- Neither of them mentions the fifth anniversary (Oct 9). It has been ten days of not mentioning it.\n24|\n25|**Net state entering Day 1:** one hidden statement, two people pretending a latch isn't crooked, $1,318 checking, and a 25-minute kitchen window scheduled for Monday 6:50pm.\n26|\n27|---\n28|\n29|# DAY 1 — Monday, October 19, 2026 — THE WEAVE\n30|*Director seat. Sources: couple_bible.md (canon), boy/answer.md, girl/answer.md, day0.md. Scene window the world granted: 6:50–7:15pm, kitchen, 1418 Delphos Ave.*\n31|\n32|## THE CLOCK\n33|- **3:30pm** — June wakes to an empty house. Gus limps after her. Doreen's voicemail (furnace; Thanksgiving) waits on the phone. The \"December\" note reads $2,110.\n34|- **4:58pm** — Marcus, still in the van from Troy, calls Wilmington Trans and approves the **$2,340** transmission: $500 down on the debit, $1,840 at Friday pickup. Two minutes before the 5pm deadline. He does the math *after*, in the Kettering lot, in silence.\n35|- **6:50pm** — The kitchen. Gus's parade. Coffee on its third reheat. Scrubs.\n36|- **6:55pm** — June states the deposit: $200 by Friday, Amara's group house, Grace's 70th on the 11th, wedding on the 12th. Facts, not a request. Marcus: \"I'll handle it.\"\n37|- **6:58pm** — June gets four words into the glovebox — \"About the car—\" — and Marcus's \"car's handled\" closes the door she was opening. She says \"fine.\" He offers gas soup instead of the anniversary.\n38|- **7:15pm** — She walks to the route 8 stop in drizzle. He texts *love you, June-bug*, types *about Friday—*, doesn't send it.\n39|- **7:40pm** — On the bus, June types *I found the statement in the glovebox*, reads it for four stops, deletes it before the hospital.\n40|\n41|## THE TWO NEAR-MISSES (the day's real event)\n42|Both of them opened the money door and both closed it themselves.\n43|- **Marcus** almost asked about the savings — the loan-kill fund — which would have meant explaining the $410/month, two years, ~$9,840 to Doreen. Drive says act, not ask. He approved the repair alone instead.\n44|- **June** almost said *I found it.* She converted the confrontation into \"fine\" and a deleted text. She knows the number; he doesn't know she knows. The statement is still hidden, and now she's carrying it in writing she erased.\n45|\n46|## LEDGER AFTER DAY 1\n47|- Checking: **$818** (after $500 transmission deposit). Coming: $1,840 repair balance Friday · $200 deposit Friday · $410 Doreen autos Thursday · $610 loan · $1,145 rent Nov 1. The math does not work without the savings, and neither has said so.\n48|- Hidden: the statement (her), the financing terms and the high quote (him), the unsent text (both, in different phones).\n49|- Unspoken: the fifth anniversary (day 10 of not mentioning it); the word \"divorce\" (never said, not even alone); \"I need help\" (not in his vocabulary).\n50|\n51|## CALIBRATION NOTES (seat telemetry)\n52|- Boy drive-law 1.0 honored: Marcus acted on every pressure (approved repair, claimed the deposit, promised the furnace) and voiced neither anger nor affection — the text instead of the sentence.\n53|- Girl drive-law 0.0 honored: June held every position — no request, no confrontation, no deleted-message sent; happiness muted, anxiety ambient, disgust at the hidden $9,840 swallowed into \"fine.\"\n54|\n55|## HOOKS INTO DAY 2 (Tue Oct 20)\n56|- June sleeps till ~2pm; Marcus on a 7am commercial rooftop in Fairborn. They pass nowhere today — the world owes them nothing until Wednesday's window.\n57|- Friday is a three-way squeeze: $1,840 pickup + $200 Atlanta deposit + Thursday's $410 already gone.\n58|- Doreen's furnace waits for Sunday; Gus's limp worsens; the $600 x-ray stays unbooked.\n59|- June's \"December\" note: $2,110 and rising. Amara will text Wednesday asking if the deposit is sent.\n60|- The crooked glovebox latch is still crooked. Both of them know it. Neither will fix it first.", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9dec8f7df9d3cd32", "name": "read_file", "step": 3}
+{"seq": 17, "ts": 1792130336.242, "role": "assistant", "content": "Canon, both Day-1 answers, and the week log are loaded. One note: the literal file `day1..day1.md` doesn't exist — the range-named deliverable on disk is `day1..day0.md` (verified), which I've read alongside `day1.md`. Registering the task, then writing the two Day-2 world inputs.", "reasoning_content": "I have everything I need. Day 2 of 7 = Tuesday, October 20, 2026.\n\nKey facts for Day 2:\n- June works Mon/Wed/Fri nights. So Tuesday night she is OFF. She worked Monday night 7p–7a, so she comes home Tuesday ~7:30am and sleeps. Tuesday she's awake from ~2pm onward (per day1.md hooks: \"June sleeps till ~2pm\").\n- Marcus: 7am commercial rooftop in Fairborn Tuesday (per day1.md hooks). Six-day weeks, on-call Saturday.\n- They pass nowhere Tuesday — per prior weave hooks. But the world is the director; I can decide what the world gives them. The prior weave said \"the world owes them nothing until Wednesday's window\" — but that was a hook/prediction, not canon. As director I can decide Day 2's shape. But I should be careful: the day1.md weave said \"The day between: they pass nowhere Tuesday — June sleeps till ~2pm, Marcus on a 7am rooftop in Fairborn [prior weave hooks]. The carry-over is a countdown on both sides: his Friday math, her Wednesday moment.\"\n\nHmm — but a day where they pass nowhere gives the persons nothing to answer. The harness runs the two people with boy_in.md/girl_in.md; each person writes their day. If they never overlap, the answers are still valid (separate interiority days). Actually that could be great: Day 2 as the \"day between\" — parallel solitudes, with the world pressing on each separately. The drama: Marcus's Friday math doesn't work; June's Wednesday moment approaches; the world can inject events that raise pressure.\n\nBut wait — should the world give them an overlap window anyway? The instruction says: \"Write boy/boy_in.md: today's situation FROM THE WORLD (what happens, where they are, the day's pressures, yesterday's fallout)\". The world decides what happens. The prior weave's hook said they pass nowhere Tuesday — but hooks are the world's own promises. Keeping continuity: the strongest choice is to honor the hook (they pass nowhere) BUT the world can still intervene via phone/text/events — a phone call is an interaction that doesn't require physical overlap. That preserves the \"inverse shifts\" tension (TIME tension) while keeping the day alive.\n\nActually, let me think about what makes Day 2 dramatic and consistent:\n\nDay 2 = Tuesday Oct 20, 2026.\n\nTimeline:\n- ~7:15am Tuesday: June arrives home from Monday night shift (7p–7a). Marcus leaves ~6:45am for the 7am Fairborn rooftop. They pass in the driveway/kitchen for maybe 2-5 minutes — the \"pass in the driveway\" beat. This is consistent with \"they pass in the driveway\" from the bible (TIME tension). The day1.md hook said \"they pass nowhere Tuesday — June sleeps till ~2pm, Marcus on a 7am rooftop\" — hmm, that says they pass NOWHERE. But physically, if she comes home at 7:15am and he leaves at 6:45am... she'd be on the bus arriving ~7:50am, he'd already be gone. Route 8, 35 minutes: she leaves hospital 7:15ish? Wait — her shift ends 7a, she walks to stop, bus 35 min → home ~7:50am. Marcus leaves for Fairborn ~6:30am (Fairborn is ~20-25 min from Dayton). So they genuinely miss each other. Good — honor the hook: they pass nowhere. He leaves a note or text; she's asleep when he's gone.\n\nActually wait — day1.md says \"June sleeps till ~2pm\" — she got home ~7:50am, sleeps till ~2pm. That's ~6 hours, plausible for a night nurse.\n\n- Marcus's day: 7am commercial rooftop in Fairborn. The world can give him: (a) a call from Doreen — furnace getting worse, maybe she tried to run it and it tripped, or she mentions something that touches the $410 secret (e.g., Doreen says \"don't worry about the storage thing, I know money's tight\" — no wait, Doreen doesn't know June found it; Doreen doesn't know about the hidden-ness. Careful: the $410 is hidden from JUNE, not from Doreen. Doreen presumably knows Marcus pays the storage unit and Visa — actually does she? The bible says Marcus \"quietly paid Doreen's storage unit and the minimum on her Visa... without telling June.\" It doesn't say whether Doreen knows. NOT IN MY CONTEXT for whether Doreen knows — I should be careful. Safest: Doreen knows Marcus \"helps\" but the specific mechanics (auto-pay from his checking) may or may not be known to her. I'll keep Doreen's knowledge vague/consistent: she knows he takes care of things; she doesn't know it's a secret from June. Actually the safest is to not have Doreen reference the payments at all.\n\nWorld events for Marcus on Tuesday (good options):\n1. Wilmington Trans calls with an update: the transmission job — maybe they found something else (axle seal / CV boot) adding cost, OR they confirm Friday pickup. Adding cost raises Friday squeeze. But careful: adding more cost might over-stack. Actually a small adder is good pressure: e.g., \"while it's in, the rear main seal is weeping — $340 more if you want it done while it's apart\" — classic mechanic upsell, real-world texture. Marcus must decide by Friday. This compounds the Friday math: $1,840 + $200 + $410 Thursday already gone + $610 loan + $1,145 rent Nov 1 vs $818 checking. The math already doesn't work. An adder makes it sharper. But maybe keep it optional — the world presents it, he decides in his answer.\n2. Doreen's furnace: she called twice Monday. Tuesday the world can escalate: overnight low in the 30s, Doreen reports the furnace stopped mid-night and she ran a space heater — now it's not just \"sounds like\" — it's failing. Marcus promised Sunday. The world can pressure: Doreen says the house was 58°F this morning. Does Marcus go earlier than Sunday? That's his choice. Also: Doreen asks him to come by — maybe Wednesday? But Wednesday is June's chosen confrontation window 